WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.871 --> 00:00:03.019
Line 3 stands in hall B.

2
00:00:03.666 --> 00:00:06.313
But the plant wants many more projects this year.

3
00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:08.645
You led that project.

4
00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:13.301
Now you advise the plant manager on which projects come next.

5
00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:16.076
That takes two stations.

6
00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:19.414
First choosing and steering, then advising.

7
00:00:20.749 --> 00:00:23.652
The first station is Programmes and governance.

8
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.628
There are more projects than people, so you choose which to do and which wait.

9
00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:31.168
Then you steer them together.

10
00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:35.221
Who decides what, and do the benefits really arrive?

11
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.841
You use a capacity line and a decision log.

12
00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:43.563
Without this everything runs at once, and less gets done.

13
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.573
The second station is The consulting craft.

14
00:00:48.315 --> 00:00:52.889
The manager pays for your advice, and it starts with a proposal about the problem.

15
00:00:53.906 --> 00:00:55.148
Then it is about trust,

16
00:00:55.299 --> 00:00:59.339
about staying independent and about work that brings new work.

17
00:01:00.396 --> 00:01:03.519
You use a diagnostic phase and the trust equation.

18
00:01:04.538 --> 00:01:08.450
Without this your advice is about yourself and not about the client.

19
00:01:09.821 --> 00:01:11.504
This is the map of Projects.

20
00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:15.665
The two stations of choosing and advising lie at the top.

21
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.723
First Programmes and governance.

22
00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:23.119
It covers programmes, priorities, steering and benefits.

23
00:01:24.192 --> 00:01:25.794
Then The consulting craft.

24
00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:29.913
It covers proposals, trust, ethics and growth.

25
00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:33.701
Each station is a module with four topics.

26
00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:35.706
Start where it hurts.
